@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@samp{redline} lets you review a prose file the way you would review a document in a word processor: you comment on passages, reply to comments in threads, suggest insertions, deletions and replacements, accept or reject those suggestions, and mark threads resolved. It is designed for working with AI agents: when you are done, you hand the file to an agent, which reads the open threads and answers them in the same file.

Everything lives in the file itself. Comments and suggestions are written as @uref{https://github.com/CriticMarkup/CriticMarkup-toolkit, CriticMarkup}, and their metadata (ids, authors, times, replies, review state) is kept in a YAML block at the end of the file. The format is @uref{https://github.com/peterhartree/roughdraft/blob/main/docs/spec/roughdraft-flavored-markdown.md, Roughdraft Flavored Markdown 0.2}, defined by @uref{https://github.com/peterhartree/roughdraft, Roughdraft}, a local Markdown review app. A Markdown file reviewed in Emacs can therefore be opened in Roughdraft and vice versa, and any agent that knows the format can take part. Because the review is ordinary text, it survives version control and any other editor.

A short example:

@example
Please revisit @{==this sentence==@}@{>>Needs a source.<<@}@{#c1@}.
Add @{++one concrete example++@}@{#s1@}.

---
comments:
  c1:
    by: user
    at: "2026-04-28T12:00:00.000Z"
  c2:
    body: I can add one from the intro.
    by: AI
    at: "2026-04-28T12:05:00.000Z"
    re: c1
suggestions:
  s1:
    by: AI
    at: "2026-04-28T12:05:00.000Z"
@end example

@samp{redline} is markup agnostic. A small @emph{adapter} tells it which text is literal (code) and where the review block lives; the rest is shared:

@itemize
@item
In Markdown buffers, markers inside fenced code blocks, code spans, YAML frontmatter and HTML comments are left alone, and the review block is the final @samp{---} YAML block, exactly as Roughdraft writes it.
@item
In Org buffers, the same CriticMarkup is used, but the YAML lives in a final @samp{#+begin_comment redline} block so that it never appears in exported output, and the markup is rewritten on export (@ref{Org files}).
@item
In any other mode, a plain adapter treats no text as literal and uses the Markdown review block.
@end itemize

The package's capabilities, by workflow:

@itemize
@item
Reviewing: comment on a region or at point, add document-level comments, reply, edit, delete and react (@ref{Commenting}).
@item
Suggesting changes and settling them (@ref{Suggesting changes}).
@item
Resolving threads and clearing resolved ones (@ref{Resolving threads}).
@item
Reading the review: hidden markup, a thread window that follows point, and a list of all threads (@ref{Display}, @ref{Threads and lists}).
@item
Handing the file to an agent (@ref{Working with agents}).
@end itemize

@samp{redline} requires Emacs 29.1 and the @samp{yaml} package from MELPA@. @samp{markdown-mode} and Org are optional.

@node Getting started
@chapter Getting started

Install the package and bind @code{redline-command-map} to a prefix key in @code{redline-mode-map}. The package deliberately binds no prefix itself: @samp{markdown-mode} and Org use most @samp{C-c C-…} keys, and @samp{C-c LETTER} keys are reserved for users.

@lisp
(use-package redline
  :bind (:map redline-mode-map
              ("C-c r" . redline-command-map)))
@end lisp

Then enable @code{redline-mode} in a Markdown or Org buffer with @samp{M-x redline-mode}, or add it to a mode hook. The key bindings below assume the prefix @samp{C-c r}.

