@node Commands
@chapter Commands

All commands that change the file share three properties. They parse the buffer afresh first, so they act on what is there now. They check that the change is safe before touching anything, and refuse with a message otherwise, leaving the buffer unchanged. And each one is a single undo step that covers both the inline markup and the review block.

@menu
* The minor mode::
* Commenting::
* Suggesting changes::
* Resolving threads::
* Navigation::
* Display::
* Threads and lists::
* Working with agents::
@end menu

@node The minor mode
@section The minor mode

@findex redline-mode
@findex redline-refresh
@code{redline-mode} is a buffer-local minor mode. Enabling it parses the buffer, draws the review display (@ref{Display}), and redraws it whenever you pause after an edit. In a buffer visiting a file it also turns on @code{auto-revert-mode} (and turns it off again when you disable @code{redline-mode}, if it was off before), so that replies an agent writes to the file appear without you reverting by hand. If you have unsaved changes when the file changes on disk, Emacs asks you what to do as it always does; @samp{redline} never merges silently.

@samp{M-x redline-refresh} reparses the buffer and redraws everything immediately. You rarely need it.

@node Commenting
@section Commenting

@findex redline-comment
With an active region, @samp{C-c r c} (@code{redline-comment}) asks for a comment and attaches it to the region: the region becomes a highlighted passage followed by the comment. Without a region, it inserts a standalone comment at point, which applies to the surrounding paragraph. With a prefix argument (@samp{C-u C-c r c}), it adds a document-level comment instead, which lives only in the review block and applies to the whole file.

The prompt accepts several lines: press @samp{C-j} or @samp{S-RET} for a newline and @samp{RET} to finish. A comment with a line break is stored as the @samp{body} of its review entry, with an empty inline marker, because a blank line inside inline markup would break the paragraph. This is how the format represents multi-paragraph comments, and Roughdraft does the same.

The command refuses a region that spans more than one paragraph, overlaps or contains existing review markup, lies in code or metadata, or touches the review block, and it refuses a region whose text contains a CriticMarkup delimiter (such as @samp{=}@}= or @samp{~>}), because the result would be ambiguous. A comment may not contain @samp{<<@}}, the comment's closing delimiter.

Every new comment gets the next free id (@samp{c1}, @samp{c2}, …), your @code{redline-author} label and the current time.

@findex redline-reply
@samp{C-c r r} (@code{redline-reply}) asks for a reply to the item at point and records it in the review block with @samp{re} pointing at its parent. Replies have no inline position; they appear in the thread window and the list (@ref{Threads and lists}), where you can reply to a reply. The item at point can be a comment, a suggestion, or a commented passage, which stands for its first comment. If the item is plain CriticMarkup without an id, @samp{redline} gives it one first, in the same change, so that the reply still points to it after you save and reopen the file.

@findex redline-edit
@samp{C-c r e} (@code{redline-edit}) lets you edit the text of the comment at point, starting from its current text. Inline comment text stays inline unless your new text has a line break, in which case it moves to the review block. A comment whose text already lives in the review block stays there. Suggestions cannot be edited; reject them and suggest again.

@findex redline-delete
@samp{C-c r d} (@code{redline-delete}) deletes the comment at point and all replies below it, asking first if there are replies. When the last comment on a highlighted passage goes, the highlight is removed too, so the passage is back to plain text. To remove a suggestion, accept or reject it instead.

@findex redline-react
@samp{C-c r +} (@code{redline-react}) sets your reaction to the comment at point: @samp{up}, @samp{down} or @samp{clarify}, or @samp{none} to clear it. Reactions are part of the format; Roughdraft shows them, and agents can read them as quick feedback. They apply to comments only.

@node Suggesting changes
@section Suggesting changes

@findex redline-suggest-insertion
@findex redline-suggest-deletion
@findex redline-suggest-replacement
@samp{C-c r i} (@code{redline-suggest-insertion}) asks for text and inserts it at point as a suggested insertion. @samp{C-c r k} (@code{redline-suggest-deletion}) marks the active region for deletion, and @samp{C-c r s} (@code{redline-suggest-replacement}) asks for new text and suggests replacing the region with it. The text itself is not changed until the suggestion is accepted. Suggestions get ids @samp{s1}, @samp{s2}, …, and the same safety checks as comments apply. Suggested text must stay within one paragraph.

@findex redline-accept
@findex redline-reject
@samp{C-c r a} (@code{redline-accept}) accepts the suggestion at point: an insertion's text is kept, a deletion's text is removed, and a replacement leaves the new text. @samp{C-c r x} (@code{redline-reject}) does the reverse. In both cases the markup, the suggestion's review entry, and any comments and replies about the suggestion are removed, since there is nothing left for them to refer to. Point can be on the suggestion or on a comment attached to it.

@findex redline-accept-all
@findex redline-reject-all
@samp{C-c r A} (@code{redline-accept-all}) and @samp{C-c r X} (@code{redline-reject-all}) settle every suggestion in the buffer after asking for confirmation. The whole operation is one undo step.

@node Resolving threads
@section Resolving threads

@findex redline-resolve
@findex redline-unresolve
@samp{C-c r R} (@code{redline-resolve}) marks the thread at point resolved by setting @samp{status: resolved} on its root. With a prefix argument it also asks for a short resolution summary, stored as @samp{resolved}. Resolved threads stay in the file and are dimmed. @samp{C-c r U} (@code{redline-unresolve}) clears the status and summary.

@findex redline-remove-resolved
@samp{C-c r D} (@code{redline-remove-resolved}) deletes every comment thread marked resolved, after confirmation, and removes the highlights they leave empty. Suggestions are never removed this way, even if marked resolved; accept or reject them instead. This mirrors the two notions in Roughdraft: the format's @samp{status: resolved} marking, and Roughdraft's @samp{resolve} command, which deletes threads.

@node Navigation
@section Navigation

@findex redline-next
@findex redline-previous
@findex redline-next-unresolved
@findex redline-previous-unresolved
@samp{C-c r n} (@code{redline-next}) and @samp{C-c r p} (@code{redline-previous}) move to the next or previous review item in the text. A comment counts at the start of its highlighted passage. @samp{C-c r N} (@code{redline-next-unresolved}) and @samp{C-c r P} (@code{redline-previous-unresolved}) skip resolved threads. At the last or first item, they report that there is no further item.

@node Display
@section Display

@findex redline-toggle-markup
In @code{redline-mode} the markup is hidden: delimiters and ids disappear, highlighted passages, insertions and deletions are shown with their faces (@ref{Faces}), and each comment is replaced by an indicator such as @samp{[c1·2]} (the comment's id and its number of replies, with @samp{✓} when resolved). Hovering over an indicator shows the comment. The review block at the end of the file is folded into a one-line summary.

When point is on an item, its raw markup is revealed so that you can see and edit it, and it is hidden again when point leaves. @samp{C-c r m} (@code{redline-toggle-markup}) switches the whole buffer between this display and the raw text. The display uses overlays only; it never changes the buffer text, and it takes precedence over Org's emphasis faces, which would otherwise style some CriticMarkup as verbatim or code (@ref{Org files}).

@node Threads and lists
@section Threads and lists

@findex redline-show-thread
@findex redline-thread-mode
@samp{C-c r t} (@code{redline-show-thread}) opens a side window showing the whole thread of the item at point: the commented passage, then each comment and reply indented under its parent, with author, time, resolution and reaction. As you move point between items in the reviewed buffer, the window follows. The buffer is in @code{redline-thread-mode}.

@findex redline-list
@findex redline-list-mode
@samp{C-c r l} (@code{redline-list}) lists every thread in the file in a table with its id, kind, author, status, reply count and text. Document-level comments and replies whose parent no longer exists are listed as threads of their own. The buffer is in @code{redline-list-mode}, and you can sort it by clicking a column header.

@findex redline-goto
@findex redline-view-refresh
Both buffers act on the reviewed file directly. On any entry, @samp{RET} (@code{redline-goto}) jumps to the passage in the file, @samp{r}, @samp{e}, @samp{d}, @samp{+}, @samp{R} and @samp{U} reply, edit, delete, react, resolve and unresolve, and @samp{a} and @samp{x} accept or reject a suggestion. This is the way to act on replies and document-level comments, which have no position in the text. @samp{g} (@code{redline-view-refresh}) redraws the buffer. If the reviewed buffer has changed since the view was drawn, a command redraws the view and asks you to try again, so that it never acts on the wrong item.

@node Working with agents
@section Working with agents

@findex redline-send-to-agent
@samp{C-c r S} (@code{redline-send-to-agent}) saves the buffer and hands the file to an AI agent. It builds a prompt that names the file and the format (with @code{redline-spec-url}), asks the agent to reply to each open thread in the review block as @samp{by: AI} with the next free id, and tells it not to apply or settle suggestions or change the prose unless a comment asks it to. For an Org file, the prompt describes the Org container. It then calls @code{redline-send-function}, or copies the prompt to the kill ring when that option is @samp{nil}.

Because @code{redline-mode} turns on @code{auto-revert-mode}, the agent's replies appear in the buffer when the agent saves the file; open the thread window or the list to read them.

An example @code{redline-send-function} for a tool that has a function to submit text to a running session:

@lisp
(defun my-redline-send (file prompt)
  "Submit PROMPT about FILE to my agent session."
  (my-agent-submit prompt (my-agent-session-for-file file)))

(setopt redline-send-function #'my-redline-send)
@end lisp

Here @samp{my-agent-submit} and @samp{my-agent-session-for-file} stand for whatever your agent package provides.

@node Org files
@chapter Org files

Org files use the same CriticMarkup and the same review data, with three differences.

First, the review block. YAML after a @samp{---} line would appear in exported Org documents, so in Org the YAML lives in a final comment block, which Org never exports:

@example
#+begin_comment redline
comments:
  c1:
    by: user
    at: "2026-04-28T12:00:00.000Z"
#+end_comment
@end example

Only a block with the parameter @samp{redline} is treated as review data. Lines of the YAML that Org could read as block syntax or a heading (those starting with @samp{*} or @samp{#+}, for instance a comment that quotes @samp{#+end_comment}) are comma-escaped the way Org escapes code in source blocks, and unescaped when read, so no comment text can end the block early.

Second, literal text. Markers are ignored inside source, example, export and comment blocks, fixed-width lines, keyword and comment lines, drawers, inline source blocks and Babel calls, and verbatim and code objects. Org would read three CriticMarkup forms (@samp{@{==x=}@}=, @samp{@{++x++@}} and @samp{@{~~a~>b~~@}}) as verbatim, strike-through and code, because @samp{@{} may precede emphasis. @samp{redline} resolves this by position: a review marker that opens outside any literal context is review markup, while one inside a verbatim or code object that opened earlier, as in @samp{\=@{++x++@}\=}, is literal text.

Third, export. @samp{redline} rewrites the markup in the export copy according to @code{redline-org-export-style} (@ref{Org export}). The rewrite happens before Org expands macros and include files and runs Babel, so text that the chosen style discards, such as a pending insertion under @code{original}, is never evaluated. A second pass handles markup that included files or macros introduce; that text has already been expanded by then. The export hooks are installed when the package is activated, and they do nothing in files without CriticMarkup.

@findex redline-org-export-rewrite
The rewrite is done by @code{redline-org-export-rewrite}, which is on both @code{org-export-before-processing-functions} and @code{org-export-before-parsing-functions}.

Roughdraft does not open Org files, so Org compatibility is with agents and with @samp{redline} itself.

@node Compatibility and limits
@chapter Compatibility and limits

@samp{redline} reads everything Roughdraft Flavored Markdown 0.2 defines: compact references with YAML endmatter, the older inline attribute blocks (@samp{@{id}``c1'' by=``AI'' at=``…''@}=), legacy @samp{@{@@id:…; by:…@@@}} comment metadata, replies, document-level comments and multi-paragraph bodies. It always writes compact references. An item with inline metadata is moved to the review block when you change it. Plain CriticMarkup without metadata is read as well, and gets an id when a command needs one.

When it rewrites the review block, @samp{redline} keeps fields it does not know, at any level, as their original YAML text, so data written by other tools survives your edits. Fields it does know are rewritten in a normalized form, much as Roughdraft rewrites the whole block, so the first edit of a hand-formatted block can produce a visible diff. A review block written in YAML flow style (@samp{@{…@}}) cannot be split this way: @samp{redline} reads it but refuses to change it, and tells you to edit it by hand. A single entry in flow style is kept verbatim and only that entry is read-only. Metadata-only entries whose marker you deleted by hand are dropped on the next rewrite; entries that carry text (replies and document-level comments) are never dropped implicitly.

Roughdraft can suggest inserting whole paragraphs. @samp{redline}'s own suggestions are limited to one paragraph.

@node Functions
@chapter Functions

@menu
* Parsing a buffer::
